{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the birth of facebook with razor-sharp dialogue, relentless pacing, and a hauntingly atmospheric score. directed by david fincher, the movie is less a biopic and more a psychological drama about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain or hero, but as a brilliant, socially awkward prodigy whose emotional detachment becomes both his strength and his downfall.\n\nthe screenplay, written by aaron sorkin, is a tour de force of rapid-fire dialogue that feels like a legal thriller wrapped in a tech origin story. every line crackles with wit and tension, turning courtroom depositions into electrifying set pieces. the narrative structure—alternating between two lawsuits and the rise of facebook—creates a compelling rhythm that keeps you hooked even when you know the outcome.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally stellar. andrew garfield brings vulnerability and rage to edward snyder, while jesse plemons and josh lawson offer quiet but potent portrayals of the winklevoss twins, whose entitlement and sense of betrayal are chillingly believable. jerry seinfeld makes a brief but memorable cameo as a venture capitalist, and jude law shines as the slick, morally ambiguous attorney who represents the plaintiffs.\n\ncinematographer jeff cronenweth uses a cold, blue-toned palette that mirrors the emotional distance of the characters, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s electronic score pulses with a sense of isolation and unease, becoming a character in its own right. the editing is precise, never wasting a frame, and the use of montages to depict coding and growth is both visually inventive and emotionally resonant.\n\nbeneath its sleek surface, the film asks profound questions: what is the price of genius? can connection be built on code? and is success worth the loneliness it demands? the social network doesn’t offer easy answers, but it forces you to sit with the discomfort of its conclusions. it’s not just a movie about facebook—it’s a mirror held up to the modern world, where relationships are curated, friendships are monetized, and the people behind the screens are often the most alone.\n\na flawless blend of style, substance, and suspense, the social network remains one of the most intelligent and enduring films of the 21st century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014625910262111574}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -4.410734163684538e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00043156370520591736}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4582989513874054}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.252384752035141}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0010349161457270384}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003972972277551889}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011789103882620111}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -1.0045183897018433}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.0847986231965479e-05}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -1.9256205558776855}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.001400800421833992}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0005934861255809665}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.31339237093925476}, {"token": " razor", "logprob": -1.3971824645996094}, {"token": "-sh", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": "arp", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": " dialogue", "logprob": -1.1996891498565674}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " relentless", "logprob": -0.6517826318740845}, {"token": " pacing", "logprob": -0.017998116090893745}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.1231340616